<template>
    <section>
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <style>
         pre code {
             background-color: #eee;
             border: 1px solid #999;
             display: block;
             padding: 20px;
         }
        </style>
        <div>
            <p>
                lp5 is a browser-based, hierarchical, text editor and preprocessor
                designed to enable a different style of creating
                software.
                The programmer uses lp5 to create a <q>web</q>
                of small, self-contained, interlinked text
                segments that include:
                code, documentation, tests, etc.
                When successful, the result is as you see here:
                a hypertext system that
                <ul>
                    <li>explains the code</li>
                    <li>contains the code</li>
                    <li>can generate the external files needed to build the code</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                What is this <q>different style</q> of programming that
                lp5 lets you explore?
                It is a style in which explanation is
                a primary goal from the very beginning;
                having to create code that is easy to explain
                often results in creating better code.
                It is a style of small parts, simply related:
                in normal code, the unit of modularity is
                essentially the function,
                but lp5 permits modularizing any piece of
                text, however small, as you will see.
                <span hidden="hidden">
                    Whereas normal programming might call for
                    breaking a large function into smaller pieces
                    for clarity, in lp5 you simply write
                    (and document) those smaller pieces separately
                    and let lp5 put them back together when
                    creating the source code the compiler will see.
                    It is a style that lets you organize for clarity,
                    rather than to meet the constraints of the
                    programming language you are using.
                </span>
            </p>
            <p>
                lp5 is <em>not</em>:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>for beginners:</b>
                        a preprocessor adds an extra level
                        of separation between the programmer
                        and other programming tools.
                        The extra complexity, e.g., in tracing
                        errors to their source (the compiler?
                        lp5? your source?) is tolerable for
                        experienced programmers, but a discouraging
                        extra burden for a beginner.
                    </li>
                    <li><b>for faster coding:</b>
                        though it may produce better code,
                        and could save time in the long run.
                        Thinking about how to explain software
                        is a significant extra constraint and takes
                        extra up-front time.
                        We might speculate that lp5 facilitates
                        better
                        <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/2960310.2960334">
                            self-regulation
                        </a>
                        during the programming process.
                    </li>
                    <li><b>"Literate Programming":</b>
                        those few
                        <a href="#2.lp5">experienced with Knuth's work</a>
                        will
                        quickly note how lp5 is similar but different.
                        For everyone else,
                        history suggests the phrase "literate programming"
                        has been extremely unhelpful in
                        promulgating some of the best
                        ideas hidden behind it,
                        so you will find that phrase spoken nevermore in
                        the lp5 documentation.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                An lp5 program consists of a series of
                <q>sections,</q>
                where each section contains documentation
                or code fragments or both
                (the section you are currently reading
                happens to contain only
                documentation, no code).
            </p>
            <p>
                The order of the sections is the order the
                author deems most conducive to explaining
                the code,
                and is independent of the order of the code
                in the final HTML output
                (which is produced by asking lp5 to <q>compile</q>).
                Most lp5 sections are quite short
                and often contain not much more than a dozen
                lines of code.
                Simple parts, simply related:
                fundamental to lp5.
            </p>
            <b>documentation fragments:</b>
            <p>
                The documentation portion of a section is
                written in normal HTML.
                The first headline element
                (<code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code>, etc.),
                if any,
                in the documentation portion of the section
                will be copied to the <q>table of contents</q>
                pane on the left,
                with appropriate indentation.
                There, you can click on the headline text of a section to scroll
                to that section of your code.
            </p>
            <b>code fragments:</b>
            <p>
                The code fragment portion of a section is written
                in a language nearly identical to HTML5,
                except that the code fragment may have a <em>name</em>,
                and the code fragment may contain references to other
                named code fragments.
            </p>
            <p>
                Unnamed code fragments form the root of a directed
                graph that is traversed to produce the final output HTML.
                As each code fragment is copied to the output,
                any reference to another code fragment causes the
                copying to recurse.
            </p>
            <p>
                Code fragments that share the same name will
                be combined together in the output in the order
                they appeared in the lp5 source sections.
                This greatly alleviates the need to think of
                unique names for each logical group of global variables,
                CSS declarations, etc.
            </p>

        </div>
        <div>
            <pre>
                <code>
int main(){
    foob();
}
                </code>
            </pre>  
        </div>
    </section>
</template>
